 {
  "key": "complex.argand-polar.q16",
  "type": "EXTENDED_RESPONSE",
  "concepts": [
   "amaths.complex.argand-polar"
  ],
  "difficulty": 4,
  "marks": 4,
  "estimatedSeconds": 360,
  "stem": "Let $z_1 = r_1(\\cos\\theta_1 + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta_1)$ and $z_2 = r_2(\\cos\\theta_2 + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta_2)$. Prove that $z_1z_2 = r_1r_2\\left(\\cos(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2) + \\mathrm{i}\\sin(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2)\\right)$, and state what this shows about $|z_1z_2|$ and $\\arg(z_1z_2)$.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "M1 expands the product, using $\\mathrm{i}^2 = -1$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "M1 groups real part $\\cos\\theta_1\\cos\\theta_2 - \\sin\\theta_1\\sin\\theta_2$ and imaginary part $\\sin\\theta_1\\cos\\theta_2 + \\cos\\theta_1\\sin\\theta_2$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p3",
      "description": "A1 applies the compound-angle formulae to reach the result.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p4",
      "description": "B1 states $|z_1z_2| = r_1r_2$ and $\\arg(z_1z_2) = \\theta_1 + \\theta_2$ (up to a multiple of $2\\pi$).",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "modelAnswer": "$$\nz_1z_2 = r_1r_2(\\cos\\theta_1 + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta_1)(\\cos\\theta_2 + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta_2)\n$$\n$$\n= r_1r_2\\left[(\\cos\\theta_1\\cos\\theta_2 - \\sin\\theta_1\\sin\\theta_2) + \\mathrm{i}(\\sin\\theta_1\\cos\\theta_2 + \\cos\\theta_1\\sin\\theta_2)\\right]\n$$\n$$\n= r_1r_2\\left[\\cos(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2) + \\mathrm{i}\\sin(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2)\\right]\n$$\nSo the modulus of the product is $r_1r_2$ and its argument is $\\theta_1 + \\theta_2$, adjusted by $2\\pi$ if needed."
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "The compound-angle formulae are exactly what make arguments add.",
  "hints": [
   "Expand the brackets and collect real and imaginary parts."
  ],
  "skill": "ANALYSIS",
  "calculator": false
 },
 {
